Two fixes to the A2091 card driver, amiga/alien/a2091.c, keeping it in
lockstep with a3091.c.patch: make the chip-mem DMA bounce PER-UNIT, closing
the same cross-unit-reselect aliasing (first section), and repair the start-
queue lifecycle across disconnect/reselect (second section).

FIRST fix -- per-unit chip-mem DMA bounce:

amiga/alien/a2091.c drives the A2091/A590 DMAC (rev. D) + WD33C93.  Like the
A3000, its DMAC cannot reach Fast RAM at or above 0x1000000, so a caller buffer
there is bounced through chip RAM: write copies caller->chip before the DMAC
reads it; read DMAs into chip then copies chip->caller when the transfer stops.

The stock driver keeps a `struct card` per host adapter and, correctly, a saved
SCSI transfer count PER UNIT (units[].tc0/tc1/tc2) because the WD33C93 runs a
disconnect/reselect DFA (service()) and targets interleave on the bus.  But the
bounce state -- the chip buffer pointer `chipaddr` and the copy-back length
`tc` -- lived in `struct card`, i.e. ONE copy shared by all eight units on the
card.  stopdma() copies `cp->tc` bytes from `cp->chipaddr` to
`cp->curunitp->comhead->addr + nbyte - cp->tc`.  case 2/3 of the DFA switch
`cp->curunitp` from one units[] entry to the reselected one and re-arm the DMAC;
once curunitp names unit B while the shared chipaddr/tc still describe unit A's
transfer (or B's startdma has overwritten them while A's copy-back is still
owed), the three operands no longer name one unit.  The copy-back then writes
one unit's buffer to another unit's address with a mismatched length, so
`addr + nbyte - tc` lands outside the target buffer -> wild write ->
`hat_pt2ptdat` panic / `User BUS ERROR`.  This is the identical latent defect
proven on the A3000's a3091.c (concurrent `dd` of one unit while `cp` writes
another); it bites the A2091 for any transfer to a buffer at or above 0x1000000.

Fix (mirrors a3091.c.patch): move the bounce state into the per-unit
`struct unit`, exactly like tc0/1/2.  Each unit gets its own `chipaddr` (the
buffer in use for THIS unit's in-flight bounced DMA, 0 otherwise) and `dma_tc`
(that DMA's byte count); the shared `struct card` fields are dropped.
startdma(cp, up) writes up->chipaddr/up->dma_tc; stopdma() reads them back
through cp->curunitp, so source buffer, destination address and length are
structurally the same unit's and no reselect that flips curunitp between units[]
can cross them.  Walk the path: startdma(A) sets A->chipaddr/A->dma_tc; a
reselect to B (case 2 stopdma -> case 3) copies A's buffer back to A using A's
own state, then startdma(B) sets B->chipaddr/B->dma_tc; B's later stopdma copies
B's buffer using B's own state.  (`cp->tc` stays in struct card unchanged as the
card-level "a DMA is running" guard the certified stop sequence relies on; it is
no longer used as a copy-back length.)

The buffer is also now a PERSISTENT per-unit chip buffer allocated ONCE per card
in initialize() instead of AllocMem'd in startdma() and FreeMem'd in stopdma()
on every high-buffer DMA -- both of which run in the a2091intr (INT2) handler,
where allocating is fragile.  It is sized BOUNCESZ = one page (0x1000):
amiga_dma_pageio() chunks every block/raw disk and tape transfer to a page, and
the only larger consumer (scsi.c GSIO, <=64 KB) bounces through a low kernel
iobuf that never trips the >=0x1000000 test, so nothing above a page reaches the
bounce; a `if (cp->tc > BOUNCESZ) panic` guard fails loud rather than
overrunning.  The `>= 0x1000000` trigger and chip-RAM semantics are unchanged;
buffers below it still go direct (up->chipaddr = 0).

SECOND fix -- start-queue lifecycle across disconnect/reselect (the same
defect fixed in a3091.c.patch, which inherited this driver's DFA skeleton;
found 2026-08-07 by instruction-level analysis of the a3091 interrupt path,
then confirmed here line-for-line):

Each card keeps a queue of units waiting to be (re)started (cp->starthead/
starttail, linked through unit.next).  service() case 2 -- a reselection
arriving while another unit's select-and-transfer is still STARTING --
correctly requeues the preempted unit (uqueue) and falls into case 3 to resume
the reselecting unit; but case 3 resumes `cp->units[reg(SI)]` WITHOUT removing
it from the start queue, and nothing else ever does.  If SI resolves to a unit
still linked there -- in particular the very unit case 2 just requeued -- that
unit is simultaneously ACTIVE (curunitp, RESUMING) and QUEUED; when its command
chain drains (case 0, no successor), the stale link survives and the next
startany() pops it: `rp = up->comhead` == 0, the CDB is fetched through the
null pointer and `dp->sac` is computed from garbage -- a wild DMA.  uqueue()'s
unconditional `up->next = 0` adds a second shape: re-linking an already-linked
unit truncates the queue and strands every unit behind it.  Only real
disconnect/reselect traffic (concurrent multi-unit I/O) can reach this.

Fix (identical to a3091.c.patch, parameterized by card): unqueue(cp, up) on the
reselect-resume path restores the invariant "linked on the start queue XOR
active on the chip" (no-op for the normal disconnected-unqueued reselector);
uqueue() refuses to double-link (new unit.onq membership flag); startany()
skips a popped entry whose comhead has drained instead of dereferencing it.
Normal-path behavior, the resume sequence (CP=0x44, saved tc0/1/2) and the
per-unit bounce state above are all untouched.

NOTE: the current bench has no emulated A2091 card, so unlike a3091.c this change
is BUILD-VALIDATED ONLY (cross-compiles clean; not exercised on hardware).  It is
included because a2091.c is the reference template a3091.c derives from and it
carries the identical bugs; keeping the two in lockstep avoids a latent trap for
anyone who runs Amix on a real A2091/A590.

Apply against a stock Amix 2.1 source tree:
    cd /usr/sys && patch -p0 < a2091.c.patch      # target: amiga/alien/a2091.c

--- amiga/alien/a2091.c.orig
+++ amiga/alien/a2091.c
@@ -12,6 +12,15 @@
 #include	"rico.h"
 #include	"sd.h"
 
+/*
+ * Largest transfer that can reach the chip-mem bounce path (a caller buffer at
+ * or above 0x1000000).  amiga_dma_pageio() chunks all block/raw disk & tape
+ * I/O to one page, and the only larger consumer (scsi.c GSIO, <=64 KB) bounces
+ * through a low kernel iobuf that never trips the >=0x1000000 test.  A guard in
+ * startdma() panics rather than overrun the buffer should that ever change.
+ */
+#define	BOUNCESZ	0x1000		/* NBPP: one 68030 page */
+
 
 struct device {
 	uchar		xxxxxxx0[0x40];
@@ -69,6 +78,10 @@
 	uchar		tc0,
 			tc1,
 			tc2;
+	ushort		*bounce,	/* persistent MEMF_CHIP bounce buffer */
+			*chipaddr;	/* == bounce while a bounced DMA runs, else 0 */
+	uint		dma_tc;		/* byte count of this unit's bounced DMA */
+	bool		onq;		/* linked on the card's start queue */
 };
 struct card {
 	volatile struct device	*device;
@@ -79,7 +92,6 @@
 	uint		istate,
 			tc,
 			startcom;
-	ushort		*chipaddr;
 	bool		initialized;
 	char		xxxxxxxx[30];
 };
@@ -179,6 +191,9 @@
 volatile struct unit	*up;
 {
 
+	if (up->onq)			/* already linked: re-linking would */
+		return;			/* clobber ->next and truncate the queue */
+	up->onq = TRUE;
 	up->next = 0;
 	if (cp->starthead) {
 		cp->starttail->next = up;
@@ -192,6 +207,36 @@
 }
 
 
+/*
+ * Unlink a unit from the card's start queue if it is on it.  Called on the
+ * reselect-resume path (case 3): a unit that case 2 just requeued can be
+ * the very unit now reselecting, and resuming it while it stays linked
+ * lets a later startany() restart it after its command chain has drained
+ * (comhead == 0 -> CDB fetched through a null pointer, wild DMA address).
+ */
+static
+unqueue( cp, up)
+volatile struct card	*cp;
+volatile struct unit	*up;
+{
+	volatile struct unit	*p;
+
+	unless (up->onq)
+		return;
+	up->onq = FALSE;
+	if (cp->starthead == up)
+		cp->starthead = up->next;
+	else
+		for (p = cp->starthead; p; p = p->next)
+			if (p->next == up) {
+				unless (p->next = up->next)
+					cp->starttail = p;
+				break;
+			}
+	up->next = 0;
+}
+
+
 static
 startany( cp)
 volatile struct card	*cp;
@@ -201,11 +246,16 @@
 	volatile struct device	*dp;
 	uint		i;
 
-	unless ((cp->istate == IDLE)
-	and (up = cp->starthead))
+	unless (cp->istate == IDLE)
 		return;
-	cp->starthead = up->next;
-	rp = up->comhead;
+	for (;;) {			/* skip entries drained while queued */
+		unless (up = cp->starthead)
+			return;
+		cp->starthead = up->next;
+		up->onq = FALSE;
+		if (rp = up->comhead)
+			break;
+	}
 	cp->curunitp = up;
 	dp = cp->device;
 	for (i=0; i<nel( rp->cdb); ++i)
@@ -270,6 +320,7 @@
 		uqueue( cp, cp->curunitp);
 	case 3:
 		up = &cp->units[reg( dp, SI)&SDUNITS-1];
+		unqueue( cp, up);	/* resuming: it must not stay startable */
 		unless (rp = up->comhead) {
 			cp->istate = badhardware( ss, cp);
 			break;
@@ -356,15 +407,19 @@
 		dp->cntr = 1<<5 | 1<<4;
 	else
 		dp->cntr = 1<<5 | 1<<4 | 1<<3;
+	up->dma_tc = cp->tc;
 	if (rp->addr >= (caddr_t)0x1000000) {
-		unless (cp->chipaddr = AllocMem( cp->tc, MEMF_CHIP))
-			panic( "a2091: no chip mem");
+		if (cp->tc > BOUNCESZ)
+			panic( "a2091: DMA too big");
+		up->chipaddr = up->bounce;
 		unless (rp->reading)
-			bcopy( rp->addr+rp->nbyte-cp->tc, cp->chipaddr, cp->tc);
-		dp->sac = cp->chipaddr;
+			bcopy( rp->addr+rp->nbyte-cp->tc, up->chipaddr, cp->tc);
+		dp->sac = up->chipaddr;
 	}
-	else
+	else {
+		up->chipaddr = 0;
 		dp->sac = rp->addr + rp->nbyte - cp->tc;
+	}
 	dp->st_dma = TRUE;
 }
 
@@ -383,12 +438,15 @@
 		;
 	dp->clr_int = TRUE;
 	dp->sp_dma = TRUE;
-	if (cp->chipaddr) {
-		volatile struct sdcom *rp = cp->curunitp->comhead;
-		if (rp->reading)
-			bcopy( cp->chipaddr, rp->addr+rp->nbyte-cp->tc, cp->tc);
-		FreeMem( cp->chipaddr, cp->tc);
-		cp->chipaddr = 0;
+	{
+		volatile struct unit	*up = cp->curunitp;
+		if (up->chipaddr) {
+			volatile struct sdcom	*rp = up->comhead;
+			if (rp->reading)
+				bcopy( up->chipaddr,
+				       rp->addr+rp->nbyte-up->dma_tc, up->dma_tc);
+			up->chipaddr = 0;
+		}
 	}
 }
 
@@ -417,6 +475,12 @@
 	setreg( dp, CON, DM2|EDI|IDI);
 	setreg( dp, SI, ER);
 	setreg( dp, TP, 0xFF);
+	{
+		int	u;
+		for (u = 0; u < SDUNITS; ++u)
+			unless (cp->units[u].bounce = AllocMem( BOUNCESZ, MEMF_CHIP))
+				panic( "a2091: no chip mem");
+	}
 }
 
 
